to-bob-coecke-source : List Word
to-bob-coecke-source = lines-of (cons "# Bob Coecke"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Bob, you have spent twenty years saying one thing in several vocabularies: the primitive of a theory is the process, the wiring between processes is the content, and what a system means is fixed by how it composes. You said it first for quantum mechanics with Samson Abramsky, then for rewriting with Ross Duncan in the ZX-calculus, then for language with Stephen Clark and Mehrnoosh Sadrzadeh in DisCoCat, and then for text itself in DisCoCirc. The fields you worked in took each result as a tool for that field: a graphical calculus for physicists, a compiler pass for quantum circuits, a curiosity for computational linguistics. The result underneath all of them is a claim about what meaning is. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your work established that the field has not caught up to"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Natural language processing settled for distribution: a word's meaning is where it sits among other words, learned from counts, and grammar is left for the network to rediscover if it can. DisCoCat refused that split. It took the distributional meanings and the grammar together and made grammar the wiring that composes them, so that a sentence's meaning is computed from its parts by the type-theoretic structure that pregroup grammar already wrote down. That is the correct relation between syntax and semantics, and the field walked past it into statistical scale."
  (cons ""
  (cons "DisCoCirc went further, and its consequence is still under-read. A text is a circuit: entities are wires, sentences are processes that act on the wires they mention, and meaning is updated as the text unfolds. With Vincent Wang-Mascianica and Jonathon Liu you showed this for a substantial fragment of English, and you stated two results that change what language is taken to be. Text circuits are generative for text: every circuit built from the generators is some text. And they are independent of word order and sentence length, so the same circuit stands behind different languages and, as you argue, behind spatial and visual cognition too. Language, on this account, is a surface notation for a structure that is not itself linguistic."
  (cons ""
  (cons "In the 2025 announcement of lambeq Gen II with Dimitri Kartsaklis, you put the practical side plainly: composition gives interpretability that can be read off the structure, and compositional generalisation makes the trainability problems of quantum models vanish. Both are the same fact seen twice. A model built compositionally is understood compositionally, and a model that generalises by composition does not depend on searching a landscape."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity is, in your terms"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik describes hyperactivity as a language and a computational model of reality, Wolfram's ruliad executing on silicon. Its computational rules follow the CCHM cubical calculus, extended with clock-indexed guarded computation"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". Types and terms are not two species of artifact, one called data and one called code"
  (cons (cite s2-p9 "(§2)")
  (cons ", and a content-addressed codebase holds programs together with their typing derivations, semantics, equivalences, transformations, dependency certificates and proofs"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". The checker is itself a term that the machine evaluates"
  (cons (cite s1-2-p2 "(§1.2)")
  (cons "; Avik's own description adds that it is consistent with Cubical Agda, and checked in Cubical Agda too."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Diagram equations as paths.** ZX is complete: two diagrams denote the same process exactly when the rewrite rules connect them. In the paper equality computes: a path is a term indexed by an interval coordinate, and transport along a path changes a dependent construction over one end into the corresponding construction over the other"
  (cons (cite s3-p11 "(§3)")
  (cons "; an equivalence supplies such a path, and for a structured equivalence the operations and the rationales for their laws move together"
  (cons (cite s1-p27 "(§1)")
  (cons ". Stated in the language, a proved equality of two diagrams is therefore a path, and every construction depending on one diagram is carried to the other by transport. Picturing Quantum Processes, which you wrote with Aleks Kissinger, already reasons this way."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**A text's readings form a family, and further text refines it.** In the paper the interpretations and updates compatible with an utterance at a participant's state form a family, and another participant or a later state can yield a different one"
  (cons (cite s1-p21 "(§1, 1.4)")
  (cons ". What an observation warrants is a construction over the fiber of histories compatible with it, and refining the observation keeps what was established"
  (cons (cite s1-p13 "(§1)")
  (cons ". These operations do not introduce probability to conceal missing information: they retain the missing distinctions and establish exactly what follows despite them"
  (cons (cite s1-p14 "(§1)")
  (cons ". An occurrence's meaning is determinate exactly when the transported family of its selected continuations is contractible"
  (cons (cite language-derived-from-the-record-source "(Part III, Language derived from the record)")
  (cons ". DisCoCirc keeps the readings and lets the rest of the text select among them; the paper gives the type of what is warranted at each step."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Meaning is received, not carried.** The paper does not assume that an expression carries a context-independent semantic object: what an expression means depends on the receiving participant, the retained history and the interpretation in use"
  (cons (cite s1-p18 "(§1)")
  (cons ". Avik is a linguist who mastered computer science, and his view is that language is trained synesthesia: a learned mapping from sound and mark onto a structure that is not itself made of words. Your result that text circuits are independent of word order and carry across languages is, on his reading, that view shown on a fragment of English."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What closes, and what it serves"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your programme has had two open edges: getting from fragments of English to all text without falling back on unstructured learned approximations, and where the circuits live, so far in a research package apart from the knowledge they describe. In the paper's account of knowledge graphs an edge is a term inhabiting the type of the relation it establishes"
  (cons (cite hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-source "(Part III, hyperactivity as the theory of knowledge graphs)")
  (cons ", and a rendering, source view or proof tree is a chosen observation of the same retained objects"
  (cons (cite s10-9-p6 "(§10.9)")
  (cons ". Text and the structure it renders are kept in one store."
  (cons ""
  (cons "It serves your quantum line as well. A process theory is a theory of what composes with what. A peer can send a definition with its dependency closure and evidence to another"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ", and a retained result is reused exactly where its dependency certificate still validates"
  (cons (cite s10-5-p26 "(§10.5)")
  (cons ", so a composition is recorded once and reused by anyone. Avik holds relational quantum mechanics to be the right resolution of physics; on that reading, your categorical quantum mechanics, processes first and states second, is the account of physics his model of reality fits."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Credit"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Every piece of your work that hyperactivity builds on is yours, credited in full and by name where it is used: categorical quantum mechanics with Samson Abramsky, the ZX-calculus with Ross Duncan, DisCoCat with Stephen Clark and Mehrnoosh Sadrzadeh, DisCoCirc and text circuits with Vincent Wang-Mascianica and Jonathon Liu, the diagrammatic method of Picturing Quantum Processes with Aleks Kissinger and of Quantum in Pictures with Stefano Gogioso, and lambeq with Dimitri Kartsaklis and the Quantinuum team. Nothing here is offered as superseding it. Read this page, and the rest of the graph, for what your work makes possible when its structure is the language rather than a tool beside it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, the blocks linked above"
  (cons "- Bob Coecke, Wikipedia (positions: Oxford until 2020/21; Cambridge Quantum and Quantinuum Chief Scientist, to 2025; Perimeter Institute Distinguished Visiting Research Chair since 2023; Wolfson College emeritus fellow), https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bob_Coecke"
  (cons "- Quantinuum, Meet Professor Bob Coecke, https://quantinuum.com/qai/bobcoecke"
  (cons "- The Mathematics of Text Structure (DisCoCirc), arXiv 1904.03478, https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.03478v1"
  (cons "- Wang-Mascianica, Liu, Coecke, Distilling Text into Circuits, arXiv 2301.10595, https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.10595v1"
  (cons "- Coecke and Kartsaklis, lambeq Gen II (Quantinuum blog, 22 May 2025), https://www.quantinuum.com/blog/lambeq-gen-ii-a-quantum-enhanced-interpretable-and-scalable-text-based-nlp-software-package"
  (cons "- Wolfson College, Demystifying the quantum world with emeritus fellow Bob Coecke, https://www.wolfson.ox.ac.uk/news/demystifying-the-quantum-world-with-emeritus-fellow-bob-coecke/"
  nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
